<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-probability-expected-value" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Expected Value</title>

	<objectives>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<p>
						Make a probability model.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						Calculate the expected value for a probability model.
					</p>
				</li>
				<li>
					<p>
						Determine whether a game is fair.
					</p>
				</li>
			</ul>
	</objectives>

	<introduction>
		<p>
			Expected value is one of the most useful ideas in probability. It has many applications, from insurance policies to making financial decisions, and it's a topic that the casinos and government agencies that run gambling operations and lotteries may hope most people never learn about!
		</p>
	</introduction>

	<subsection xml:id="subsec-probability-models">
		<title>Probability Models</title>
		<p>
			Before we can compute an expected value, we need to understand what a probability distribution (also called a probability model) is. The example below will help illustrate this concept and show how a distribution might arise. We will then use this example to introduce and develop the idea of expected value.
		</p>
		<p>
			Suppose you are commissioned to design a game for the annual College Place Block Party to raise money for the Blue Mountain Humane Society. In your proposed game, players roll a six-sided die. If they roll a 6 on the first try, they win $10. If they roll any other number, they get a second chance. 
			On the second roll, if they roll a 6, they win $3. If they fail to roll a 6 on the second attempt, they lose. With the game design complete, your next task is to determine how much to charge players in order to ensure a profit over the long run.
		</p>
		<p>
			To make a profit, the organizers need to know how much they will pay in winnings, on average, over the long run and charge more than that amount. In other words, they must charge more than the <em>expected value</em> of the game. To figure out the average winnings, you need to tabulate all the possible outcomes of the game.
		</p>
		<p>
			One way to do this is with a probability model. A <term>probability model</term><idx><h>probability model</h><see>probability distribution</see></idx> or <term>probability distribution</term><idx>probability distribution</idx><idx><h>probability</h><h>distribution</h></idx> is a table listing all possible outcomes and their corresponding probabilities. The outcomes are the amounts a player can win, and we will calculate the probabilities using what we have learned about theoretical probability.
		</p>
		<p>
			Here is the probability model for the Block Party game. Note that the probabilities in a probability distribution must add to 1.
		</p>
		<tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
			<row header="yes">
				<cell>Outcome ($ won)</cell>
				<cell>Rolling Event</cell>
				<cell>Probability</cell>
			</row>
			<row>
				<cell>$10</cell>
				<cell>Roll a 6 on the first roll</cell>
				<cell><m>P(\text{roll a 6})=\frac{1}{6}</m></cell>
			</row>
			<row>
				<cell>$3</cell>
				<cell><line>Roll not a 6 on the first roll</line><line>and a 6 on the second roll</line></cell>
				<cell><m>P(\text{roll not a 6 then roll a 6})=\frac{5}{6}\cdot\frac{1}{6}=\frac{5}{36}</m></cell>
			</row>
			<row>
				<cell>$0</cell>
				<cell><line>Roll not a 6 on the first roll</line><line>and not a 6 on the second roll</line></cell>
				<cell><m>P(\text{roll not a 6 then not a 6})=\frac{5}{6}\cdot\frac{5}{6}=\frac{25}{36}</m></cell>
			</row>
		</tabular>
	</subsection>
	<subsection xml:id="subsec-probability-expected-val">
		<title>Expected Value</title>
		<p>
			Think of the expected value as a weighted average. In the Block Party example above, we could take the average of $10, $3, and $0, but they are not all equally likely. It is much more likely to win $0 than it is to win $10. So, to find the average, we multiply each outcome by the chance it will happen and add the products together. This is the expected value of the game.
		</p>
		<assemblage xml:id="assemblage-expected-value">
			<title>Expected Value</title>
			<p>
				To find the <term>expected value</term><idx>expected value</idx><idx><h>probability</h><h>expected value</h></idx> of an experiment, multiply each possible outcome by its probability and sum the products.
			</p>
		</assemblage>
		<p>
			For the Block Party example we have:
			<md>
				<mrow> \text{Expected winnings} \amp= \$10\left(\frac{1}{6}\right)+\$3\left(\frac{5}{36}\right)+\$0\left(\frac{25}{36}\right) </mrow>
				<mrow> \amp \approx\$2.08 </mrow>
			</md>
			This tells us that, over the long run, players can expect to win $2.08 per game. This also means that the game organizer will be paying out an average of $2.08 per game. Since the game is intended to be a fundraiser, the organizers need to charge <em>more</em> than $2.08 per game in order to raise money.
		</p>
		<p>
			If the game organizers charge exactly the expected value (about $2.08) to play, the game is considered a <term>fair game</term><idx>fair game</idx> since the expected net winnings would be $0. In a fair game, the player isn't expected to win anything, nor is the owner expected to earn anything over the long run. However, if organizers charge the player more than $2.08 to play, they will earn money over the long run.
		</p>
		<p>
			Suppose you suggest charging $5 to play the game. We can determine the expected net winnings by subtracting the $5 the player has to pay from their expected winnings. This gives us:
			<md>
				<mrow>\text{Expected net winnings}\amp=\$2.08-\$5</mrow>
				<mrow> \amp=-\$2.92. </mrow>
			</md>
			This means that, over the long run, players can expect to lose an average of $2.92 per game, while the organizers can expect to raise an average of $2.92 per game.
		</p>
		<example>
			<statement>
				<p>
					A state lottery sells a $1 ticket with the prizes and probabilities shown below. (This game is made up, but its payouts are similar to those of real lottery games.) Determine the player's expected net winnings.
				</p>
				<tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row header="yes">
						<cell>Prize ($)</cell>
						<cell>Probability</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>$50</cell>
						<cell><m>\sfrac{1}{417}</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>$100</cell>
						<cell><m>\sfrac{1}{833}</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>$200</cell>
						<cell><m>\sfrac{1}{1{,}667}</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>$300</cell>
						<cell><m>\sfrac{1}{2{,}500}</m></cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The provided table is not quite a complete probability distribution since it is missing one important outcome: when the player loses. In that case the prize is $0, so we need to add a row for it. Since losing is the complement to winning <em>something</em>, the probability will be:
				</p>
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow>P(\text{Win }\$0)\amp=1-\left(\frac{1}{417}+\frac{1}{833}+\frac{1}{1{,}667}+\frac{1}{2{,}500}\right)</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp\approx 1-0.0046</mrow>
						<mrow>\amp=0.9954.</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
				
				<p>
					Adding this information to the table gives a complete probability distribution. Now we can see that players are going to lose more than 99% of the time, so the expected value will be heavily weighted toward winning $0.
				</p>

				<tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row header="yes">
						<cell>Prize ($)</cell>
						<cell>Probability</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>$50</cell>
						<cell><m>\sfrac{1}{417}</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>$100</cell>
						<cell><m>\sfrac{1}{833}</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>$200</cell>
						<cell><m>\sfrac{1}{1{,}667}</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>$300</cell>
						<cell><m>\sfrac{1}{2{,}500}</m></cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>$0</cell>
						<cell>0.9954</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
				<p>
					<md>
					<mrow>\text{Expected winnings}\amp=\$50\left(\frac{1}{417}\right)+\$100\left(\frac{1}{833}\right)</mrow>
					<mrow>\amp +\$200\left(\frac{1}{1{,}667}\right)+\$300\left(\frac{1}{2{,}500}\right) </mrow>
					<mrow>\amp +\$0(0.9954)</mrow>
					<mrow>\amp \approx \$0.12+\$0.12+\$0.12+\$0.12+\$0</mrow>
					<mrow>\amp =\$0.48.</mrow>
				</md>
				</p>
				<p>
					Therefore, the player's expected winnings are $0.48 on average over the long run. To find the expected <em>net</em> winnings, we subtract the cost to play. Since it costs $1 to play,
				</p>
				<p>
					<md>
					<mrow>\text{Expected net winnings}= \$0.48 - \$1.00 = -\$0.52.</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
				<p>
					Over the long run, a player can expect to lose about 52 cents for every dollar spent on this game. Play a million times, and you can expect to lose about $520,000! That 52 cents per ticket is what the lottery keeps, on average, to pay its costs and fund state programs.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>
		<p>
			In general, if the expected value of a game is negative, it is not a good idea to play, since in the long run you will lose money. It would be better to play a game with a positive expected value (good luck trying to find one!), although keep in mind that even if the average winnings are positive it could be the case that most people lose money and one very fortunate individual wins a great deal of money.
		</p>
		<p>
			Not surprisingly, the expected value for casino games is always negative for the player, and therefore positive for the casino. It must be positive for the casino, or they would go out of business! Players just need to keep in mind that when they play a game repeatedly, they should expect to lose money. That is fine so long as you enjoy playing the game and think it is worth the cost, but it would be wrong to expect to come out ahead.
		</p>
		<p>
			Expected value is used not only to determine the average amount won and lost at casinos and carnivals but also in business and insurance. Let's look at an example of each.
		</p>
		<example>
			<statement>
				<p>
					For 3 months, a coffee shop tracked its morning sales of coffee between 6 a.m. and 10 a.m. The following results were recorded:
				</p>
					<tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
						<row>
							<cell>Number of cups sold</cell>
							<cell>145</cell>
							<cell>150</cell>
							<cell>155</cell>
							<cell>160</cell>
							<cell>170</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>Probability</cell>
							<cell>0.15</cell>
							<cell>0.22</cell>
							<cell>0.37</cell>
							<cell>0.19</cell>
							<cell>0.07</cell>
						</row>
					</tabular>
				<p>
					How many cups of coffee should they expect to sell each morning?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					In this case the table tells us that 15% of the time they sell 145 cups of coffee between 6 a.m. and 10 a.m., 22% of the time they sell 150 cups, 37% of the time they sell 155 cups, 19% of the time they sell 160 cups, and 7% of the time they sell 170 cups. Since the highest probability is associated with 155 cups, the expected value should lie somewhat close to this.
				</p>
				<p>
					To find the expected number of coffees sold, we multiply each number of cups of coffee by its respective probability and then add the products.
				</p>
				<p>
					<md>
						<mrow> \text{Expected number of coffees sold}\amp=145(0.15)+150(0.22)</mrow>
						<mrow> \amp +155(0.37)+160(0.19)</mrow>
						<mrow> \amp +170(0.07)</mrow>
						<mrow> \amp=154.4\text{ cups of coffee.}</mrow>
					</md>
					This means that over the long run, the coffee shop can expect, on average, to sell around 154 cups of coffee each morning. This is an important tool for businesses since it helps inform them how much stock they should keep on hand.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>
		<example>
			<statement>
				<p>
					On average, a 40-year-old man in the US has a 0.3115% chance of dying in the next year<fn>According to the Social Security Administration's Actuarial Life Table, <url href="https://www.ssa.gov/oact/STATS/table4c6.html" visual="ssa.gov/oact/STATS/table4c6.html"/>.</fn>. An insurance company charges $350 annually for a life insurance policy that pays a $100,000 death benefit. What is the expected value for the insurance company on this policy?
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The first thing we want to do is organize the probabilities and outcomes in a probability distribution table. There are two outcomes<mdash/>either the policyholder dies, and the insurance company pays the benefit, or the policyholder does not die, and the company pays nothing. 
				</p>
				<p>
					The probability of paying the death benefit is equal to the chance of the person dying in the next year, and the probability of paying nothing is equal to the complement of the chance of dying in the next year.
				</p>
					<tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
						<row header="yes">
							<cell>Insurance payout</cell>
							<cell>Probability</cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>$100,000</cell>
							<cell><m>0.003115</m></cell>
						</row>
						<row>
							<cell>$0</cell>
							<cell><m>1-0.003115=0.996885</m></cell>
						</row>
					</tabular>
				<p>
					Then we can calculate:
				</p>
				<p>
					<md>
					<mrow>\text{Expected payout}\amp=\$100{,}000(0.003115)+\$0(0.996885)</mrow>
					<mrow>\amp =\$311.50.</mrow>
					</md>
				</p>
				<p>
					So, the expected payout for the insurance company is $311.50, but they are charging $350 for the policy. Their expected net revenue is
				</p>
				<p>
					<md>
					<mrow>\text{Expected net revenue}\amp=\$350-\$311.50</mrow>
					<mrow> \amp=\$38.50.</mrow>
				</md>
				</p>
				<p>
					The insurance company is making, on average, $38.50 per policy per year. This isn't surprising: the company has overhead costs and can only afford to offer policies if it makes money on them, on average. But how much money should it make? As a consumer, you should know about expected value.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>
	</subsection>

	<exercises>
		<!-- Sources: The author does not teach this section in MATH 105 at
		Walla Walla University, so these exercises are not drawn from his
		course materials. All were generated by AI (Fall 2026) and checked
		by the author.
		-->
		<title>Exercises</title>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-expected-valid-models">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Each list below gives the possible outcomes of an experiment and their probabilities. Decide whether each is a valid probability distribution. If it is not, explain why.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>Outcomes 1, 2, 3 with probabilities 0.2, 0.3, 0.5.</p></li>
						<li><p>Outcomes 1, 2, 3 with probabilities 0.4, 0.4, 0.3.</p></li>
						<li><p>Outcomes 1, 2, 3 with probabilities 0.5, <m>-0.1</m>, 0.6.</p></li>
						<li><p>Outcomes 1, 2, 3 with probabilities <m>\frac{1}{3}</m>, <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>, <m>\frac{1}{6}</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p>Outcomes 0, 1, 2, 3 with probabilities 0.15, 0.40, <m>p</m>, 0.10. Find the value of <m>p</m> that makes this a valid probability distribution.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>Valid: every probability is between 0 and 1, and <m>0.2 + 0.3 + 0.5 = 1</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p>Not valid: <m>0.4 + 0.4 + 0.3 = 1.1</m>, but the probabilities must add to 1.</p></li>
						<li><p>Not valid: a probability cannot be negative. (The probabilities do add to 1, but that is not enough.)</p></li>
						<li><p>Valid: <m>\frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{6} = \frac{2}{6} + \frac{3}{6} + \frac{1}{6} = 1</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>p = 1 - (0.15 + 0.40 + 0.10) = 1 - 0.65 = 0.35</m>.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-expected-food-truck-line">
			<statement>
				<p>
					A food truck owner tracked the number of customers waiting in line at exactly noon over several months. The results are shown below.
				</p>
				<tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row>
						<cell>Customers in line</cell>
						<cell>0</cell>
						<cell>1</cell>
						<cell>2</cell>
						<cell>3</cell>
						<cell>4</cell>
					</row>
					<row>
						<cell>Probability</cell>
						<cell>0.10</cell>
						<cell>0.25</cell>
						<cell>0.30</cell>
						<cell>0.20</cell>
						<cell>0.15</cell>
					</row>
				</tabular>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>Find the expected number of customers in line at noon.</p></li>
						<li><p>No day can have a fractional number of customers in line. What does your answer to (a) mean?</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p><m>0(0.10) + 1(0.25) + 2(0.30) + 3(0.20) + 4(0.15) = 0 + 0.25 + 0.60 + 0.60 + 0.60 = 2.05</m> customers.</p></li>
						<li><p>The expected value is a long-run average. Over many days, the line at noon averages about 2.05 customers, even though on any single day the line has a whole number of customers.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-expected-two-coins">
			<statement>
				<p>
					In a carnival game, a player flips two fair coins. The player wins $5 for two heads, $1 for exactly one head, and nothing for no heads.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>Make a probability model for the amount won.</p></li>
						<li><p>Find the expected winnings.</p></li>
						<li><p>If it costs $2 to play, find the expected net winnings.</p></li>
						<li><p>What should the game cost for it to be a fair game?</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								The four equally likely outcomes are HH, HT, TH, and TT.
							</p>
							<tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell>Outcome ($ won)</cell>
									<cell>Probability</cell>
								</row>
								<row><cell>$5</cell><cell><m>\frac{1}{4}</m></cell></row>
								<row><cell>$1</cell><cell><m>\frac{2}{4} = \frac{1}{2}</m></cell></row>
								<row><cell>$0</cell><cell><m>\frac{1}{4}</m></cell></row>
							</tabular>
						</li>
						<li><p><m>\$5\left(\frac{1}{4}\right) + \$1\left(\frac{1}{2}\right) + \$0\left(\frac{1}{4}\right) = \$1.25 + \$0.50 = \$1.75</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>\$1.75 - \$2.00 = -\$0.25</m>. Over the long run, a player loses about 25 cents per game.</p></li>
						<li><p>$1.75, the expected winnings, so that the expected net winnings are $0.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-expected-gold-spinner">
			<statement>
				<p>
					A school carnival game uses a spinner with four equal sections, one of which is gold. If the spinner lands on gold on the first spin, the player wins $8. Otherwise, the player spins again and wins $4 if the second spin lands on gold. If neither spin lands on gold, the player wins nothing.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>Make a probability model for the amount won.</p></li>
						<li><p>Find the expected winnings.</p></li>
						<li><p>The carnival charges $3 to play. Find the player's expected net winnings, and explain what this means for the carnival.</p></li>
						<li><p>What price would make this a fair game?</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell>Outcome ($ won)</cell>
									<cell>Spinning event</cell>
									<cell>Probability</cell>
								</row>
								<row><cell>$8</cell><cell>Gold on the first spin</cell><cell><m>\frac{1}{4}</m></cell></row>
								<row><cell>$4</cell><cell>Not gold, then gold</cell><cell><m>\frac{3}{4}\cdot\frac{1}{4} = \frac{3}{16}</m></cell></row>
								<row><cell>$0</cell><cell>Not gold, then not gold</cell><cell><m>\frac{3}{4}\cdot\frac{3}{4} = \frac{9}{16}</m></cell></row>
							</tabular>
						</li>
						<li><p><m>\$8\left(\frac{1}{4}\right) + \$4\left(\frac{3}{16}\right) + \$0\left(\frac{9}{16}\right) = \$2.00 + \$0.75 = \$2.75</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>\$2.75 - \$3.00 = -\$0.25</m>. Players lose an average of 25 cents per game, so the carnival raises an average of 25 cents per game.</p></li>
						<li><p>$2.75.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-expected-ball-draw">
			<statement>
				<p>
					A club plans a fundraising game. A bag holds 3 red balls and 2 blue balls. A player draws two balls without replacement. Two red balls win $5, two blue balls win $10, and one of each color wins nothing.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>Make a probability model for the amount won.</p></li>
						<li><p>Find the expected winnings.</p></li>
						<li><p>The club plans to charge $2 to play. Is this a good plan for a fundraiser? Explain.</p></li>
						<li><p>What is the least the club must charge so that it expects to raise money?</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								By the general multiplication rule, <m>P(\text{two red}) = \frac{3}{5}\cdot\frac{2}{4} = \frac{6}{20} = \frac{3}{10}</m> and <m>P(\text{two blue}) = \frac{2}{5}\cdot\frac{1}{4} = \frac{2}{20} = \frac{1}{10}</m>. By the complement rule, <m>P(\text{one of each}) = 1 - \frac{3}{10} - \frac{1}{10} = \frac{6}{10}</m>.
							</p>
							<tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell>Outcome ($ won)</cell>
									<cell>Probability</cell>
								</row>
								<row><cell>$5</cell><cell><m>\frac{3}{10}</m></cell></row>
								<row><cell>$10</cell><cell><m>\frac{1}{10}</m></cell></row>
								<row><cell>$0</cell><cell><m>\frac{6}{10}</m></cell></row>
							</tabular>
						</li>
						<li><p><m>\$5(0.3) + \$10(0.1) + \$0(0.6) = \$1.50 + \$1.00 = \$2.50</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p>No. The expected net winnings are <m>\$2.50 - \$2.00 = \$0.50</m>, so players gain an average of 50 cents per game and the club loses an average of 50 cents per game.</p></li>
						<li><p>The club must charge more than $2.50.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-expected-raffle">
			<statement>
				<p>
					A charity sells all 500 tickets in a raffle at $5 each. One ticket wins $500, two tickets win $250 each, and five tickets win $50 each. Winning tickets are drawn without replacement, so no ticket wins more than one prize.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>Make a probability model for the amount one ticket wins. Don't forget the tickets that win nothing.</p></li>
						<li><p>Find the expected winnings and the expected net winnings for one ticket.</p></li>
						<li><p>How much money does the charity raise from the raffle? How does this compare with your answer to (b)?</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Eight tickets win a prize, so <m>500 - 8 = 492</m> tickets win nothing.
							</p>
							<tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell>Outcome ($ won)</cell>
									<cell>Probability</cell>
								</row>
								<row><cell>$500</cell><cell><m>\frac{1}{500}</m></cell></row>
								<row><cell>$250</cell><cell><m>\frac{2}{500}</m></cell></row>
								<row><cell>$50</cell><cell><m>\frac{5}{500}</m></cell></row>
								<row><cell>$0</cell><cell><m>\frac{492}{500}</m></cell></row>
							</tabular>
						</li>
						<li><p>Expected winnings: <m>\$500\left(\frac{1}{500}\right) + \$250\left(\frac{2}{500}\right) + \$50\left(\frac{5}{500}\right) + \$0\left(\frac{492}{500}\right) = \$1.00 + \$1.00 + \$0.50 = \$2.50</m>. Expected net winnings: <m>\$2.50 - \$5.00 = -\$2.50</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p>The charity takes in <m>500 \times \$5 = \$2{,}500</m> and pays out <m>\$500 + 2(\$250) + 5(\$50) = \$1{,}250</m>, so it raises $1,250. That is <m>500 \times \$2.50</m>: the $2.50 each ticket holder loses on average is exactly what the charity raises per ticket.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-expected-roulette">
			<statement>
				<p>
					An American roulette wheel has 38 equally likely pockets: 18 red, 18 black, and 2 green.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>You bet $1 on red. If the ball lands in a red pocket, you get back $2 (your dollar plus $1 in winnings); otherwise you get back nothing. Find the expected amount you get back and your expected net winnings.</p></li>
						<li><p>You bet $1 on a single number instead. If the ball lands on that number, you get back $36; otherwise you get back nothing. Find your expected net winnings.</p></li>
						<li><p>About how much should you expect to lose if you make 1,000 one-dollar bets on red?</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>Expected amount back: <m>\$2\left(\frac{18}{38}\right) + \$0\left(\frac{20}{38}\right) = \frac{\$36}{38} \approx \$0.947</m>. Expected net winnings: <m>\$0.947 - \$1.00 \approx -\$0.053</m>, a loss of about 5.3 cents per dollar bet.</p></li>
						<li><p>Expected amount back: <m>\$36\left(\frac{1}{38}\right) \approx \$0.947</m>, so the expected net winnings are again about <m>-\$0.053</m>. Both bets favor the casino by the same amount.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>1{,}000 \times \$0.053 \approx \$53</m>. (Using the exact value <m>\frac{2}{38}</m> per bet gives about $52.63.)</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-expected-fair-prize">
			<statement>
				<p>
					A game costs $2 to play. The player rolls two fair dice and wins a prize if the sum is 7; otherwise the player wins nothing.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>What prize would make this a fair game?</p></li>
						<li><p>If the prize is $10, find the player's expected net winnings.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Six of the 36 equally likely outcomes have a sum of 7, so the probability of winning is <m>\frac{6}{36} = \frac{1}{6}</m>.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>If the prize is <m>x</m> dollars, the expected winnings are <m>x\left(\frac{1}{6}\right)</m>. The game is fair when this equals the $2 cost: <m>\frac{x}{6} = 2</m>, so <m>x = \$12</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>\$10\left(\frac{1}{6}\right) - \$2 \approx \$1.67 - \$2.00 = -\$0.33</m>.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-expected-phone-plan">
			<statement>
				<p>
					A phone protection plan costs $96 per year. Each year, 12% of customers on the plan file a claim, and each claim costs the company $500 to replace the phone. Assume no customer files more than one claim per year.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>Find the company's expected payout per customer per year.</p></li>
						<li><p>Find the company's expected net revenue per customer per year.</p></li>
						<li><p>What annual price would make the plan fair?</p></li>
						<li><p>The plan has a negative expected value for the customer. Give a reason a customer might still buy it.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p><m>\$500(0.12) + \$0(0.88) = \$60</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>\$96 - \$60 = \$36</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p>$60, the expected payout.</p></li>
						<li><p>Answers will vary. Expected value describes the long-run average, but a customer only lives through one year at a time. Someone who could not easily pay $500 for a new phone may prefer a certain cost of $96 to a 12% risk of a $500 expense.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-expected-renters-insurance">
			<statement>
				<p>
					An insurance company sells a renters insurance policy for $180 per year. Each year, a policyholder has a 0.02 probability of a $5,000 theft claim, a 0.005 probability of a $20,000 fire claim, and otherwise makes no claim.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>Make a probability model for the company's payout.</p></li>
						<li><p>Find the company's expected payout and expected net revenue per policy.</p></li>
						<li><p>What is the smallest annual premium the company could charge without expecting to lose money?</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row header="yes">
									<cell>Insurance payout</cell>
									<cell>Probability</cell>
								</row>
								<row><cell>$5,000</cell><cell>0.02</cell></row>
								<row><cell>$20,000</cell><cell>0.005</cell></row>
								<row><cell>$0</cell><cell><m>1 - 0.02 - 0.005 = 0.975</m></cell></row>
							</tabular>
						</li>
						<li><p>Expected payout: <m>\$5{,}000(0.02) + \$20{,}000(0.005) + \$0(0.975) = \$100 + \$100 = \$200</m>. Expected net revenue: <m>\$180 - \$200 = -\$20</m>, so the company expects to lose $20 per policy.</p></li>
						<li><p>$200. At that price the company expects to break even; to cover its overhead and make money, it must charge more.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-expected-food-truck-location">
			<statement>
				<p>
					A food truck owner is choosing where to park on Saturday. The forecast gives a 60% chance of sun and a 40% chance of rain.
					<ul>
						<li><p>At the farmers market, the owner expects a profit of $600 if it is sunny. If it rains, the market is nearly empty, and after paying the booth fee the owner loses $100.</p></li>
						<li><p>Downtown, the owner expects a profit of $350 if it is sunny and $250 if it rains.</p></li>
					</ul>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>Find the expected profit at each location.</p></li>
						<li><p>Which location has the higher expected profit? Give a reason the owner might choose the other one anyway.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>Farmers market: <m>\$600(0.6) + (-\$100)(0.4) = \$360 - \$40 = \$320</m>. Downtown: <m>\$350(0.6) + \$250(0.4) = \$210 + \$100 = \$310</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p>The farmers market, by $10. But downtown is much less risky: the owner makes at least $250 rain or shine, while the market risks a loss. An owner who cannot afford a bad day might reasonably choose downtown.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-expected-positive-lottery">
			<statement>
				<p>
					A lottery ticket costs $2 and has a 1 in 1,000,000 chance of winning a $3,000,000 jackpot; otherwise it wins nothing. (Assume the jackpot is not taxed or shared with other winners.)
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>Find the expected net winnings for one ticket.</p></li>
						<li><p>Is buying a ticket a good idea? Explain, using what expected value does and does not tell you.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>Expected winnings: <m>\$3{,}000{,}000\left(\frac{1}{1{,}000{,}000}\right) = \$3</m>. Expected net winnings: <m>\$3 - \$2 = \$1</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p>Answers will vary. The expected value is positive, but it describes the average over an enormous number of tickets. For one ticket, the probability of losing $2 is 0.999999: almost every buyer loses, and one very fortunate buyer wins a great deal. (Real jackpots are also taxed and often shared, which lowers the expected value.)</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-expected-eggs">
			<statement>
				<p>
					A backyard farmer recorded how many eggs their hens laid each day for 50 days. They got 3 eggs on 10 days, 4 eggs on 25 days, and 5 eggs on 15 days.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>Use these results to make a probability model for the number of eggs laid in a day.</p></li>
						<li><p>Find the expected number of eggs per day.</p></li>
						<li><p>About how many eggs should the farmer expect in 30 days?</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Use the experimental probabilities.
							</p>
							<tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
								<row>
									<cell>Eggs</cell>
									<cell>3</cell>
									<cell>4</cell>
									<cell>5</cell>
								</row>
								<row>
									<cell>Probability</cell>
									<cell><m>\frac{10}{50} = 0.2</m></cell>
									<cell><m>\frac{25}{50} = 0.5</m></cell>
									<cell><m>\frac{15}{50} = 0.3</m></cell>
								</row>
							</tabular>
						</li>
						<li><p><m>3(0.2) + 4(0.5) + 5(0.3) = 0.6 + 2.0 + 1.5 = 4.1</m> eggs per day.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>30 \times 4.1 = 123</m> eggs.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-expected-guessing-penalty">
			<statement>
				<p>
					On a test, each multiple-choice question has four choices. A correct answer earns 1 point, a wrong answer loses <m>\frac{1}{4}</m> point, and a blank earns 0 points.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>Find the expected score for a question if you guess at random among all four choices.</p></li>
						<li><p>Find the expected score if you can eliminate one choice you know is wrong and guess among the other three.</p></li>
						<li><p>Is it worth guessing on this test? Explain.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p><m>1\left(\frac{1}{4}\right) + \left(-\frac{1}{4}\right)\left(\frac{3}{4}\right) = \frac{1}{4} - \frac{3}{16} = \frac{1}{16} = 0.0625</m> points.</p></li>
						<li><p><m>1\left(\frac{1}{3}\right) + \left(-\frac{1}{4}\right)\left(\frac{2}{3}\right) = \frac{1}{3} - \frac{1}{6} = \frac{1}{6} \approx 0.1667</m> points.</p></li>
						<li><p>Yes. Both expected values are greater than 0, the score for a blank, so guessing raises your score on average. It pays more if you can eliminate a wrong choice first.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-expected-scratch-ticket">
			<statement>
				<p>
					A $2 scratch ticket has the prizes and probabilities shown below. Each ticket wins at most one prize.
				</p>
				<tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" left="minor" right="minor" bottom="minor">
					<row header="yes">
						<cell>Prize ($)</cell>
						<cell>Probability</cell>
					</row>
					<row><cell>$2</cell><cell><m>\sfrac{1}{5}</m></cell></row>
					<row><cell>$10</cell><cell><m>\sfrac{1}{50}</m></cell></row>
					<row><cell>$100</cell><cell><m>\sfrac{1}{1{,}000}</m></cell></row>
				</tabular>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p>Complete the probability distribution by finding the probability of winning nothing.</p></li>
						<li><p>Find the expected winnings and the expected net winnings.</p></li>
						<li><p>About one ticket in five <q>wins</q> $2. What is the net result for that player?</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li><p><m>1 - \left(\frac{1}{5} + \frac{1}{50} + \frac{1}{1{,}000}\right) = 1 - (0.2 + 0.02 + 0.001) = 0.779</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p>Expected winnings: <m>\$2(0.2) + \$10(0.02) + \$100(0.001) + \$0(0.779) = \$0.40 + \$0.20 + \$0.10 = \$0.70</m>. Expected net winnings: <m>\$0.70 - \$2.00 = -\$1.30</m>.</p></li>
						<li><p>The player just gets the $2 ticket price back, for a net result of $0. These <q>wins</q> feel good but do not make the ticket a better deal.</p></li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>
	</exercises>
</section>